# Canonical implementation policy

This repository uses Git history as the rollback mechanism.
Do NOT preserve obsolete runtime code merely to make rollback possible.

However, obsolete-looking code is not permission for automatic deletion.
All cleanup must follow `.cursor/rules/dead-code-cleanup.mdc`.

## Canonical decisions

When a new implementation has been explicitly accepted as canonical:

1. Treat the canonical implementation as the only active source of truth.
2. Do not keep old implementations commented out or reintroduce behavior from Git history unless the user explicitly requests restoration.
3. Do not create parallel helpers, fallback branches, duplicate business logic, or competing implementations when the canonical path can be extended.
4. Superseded, experimental, transitional, placeholder, compatibility, duplicate, or dead-looking code must be reported as a cleanup candidate, not deleted automatically.
5. Required compatibility code must be preserved until its requirement is explicitly proven obsolete.

Git history is the archive. The runtime source tree should converge toward one canonical implementation, but cleanup must be deliberate and verified.

## Before adding a new implementation

Search for an existing canonical implementation first.

Prefer:
- extending the canonical implementation;
- reusing existing business rules;
- recording superseded alternatives as cleanup candidates;

over:
- creating parallel helpers;
- adding another fallback branch;
- preserving two competing implementations.

## Single source of truth

Business rules that affect multiple UI surfaces must have one canonical derived model/helper.

Do not independently reproduce the same business-state interpretation in multiple pages/components.

When duplicate implementations are found:
- determine which implementation is canonical;
- identify all callers and runtime entry points;
- propose migration of callers if needed;
- record the obsolete implementation as a cleanup candidate;
- do not remove it automatically.

## Legacy code classification

Whenever modifying an existing subsystem, classify relevant old code as:

A. CANONICAL — keep and extend.
B. REQUIRED COMPATIBILITY — keep, document why and under what condition it may be removed.
C. TRANSITIONAL — cleanup candidate once its migration/rollout condition is complete.
D. DEAD / SUPERSEDED — cleanup candidate requiring verification before removal.

Agents are NOT authorized to automatically delete C or D code.
Detection may be automatic; deletion requires explicit approval and the safety process defined in `dead-code-cleanup.mdc`.

Do not delete B code without proving that its compatibility requirement has ended.

## UI experiments and placeholders

Temporary UI, mock payment states, coming-soon placeholders, experimental flows, and transitional UX should not survive indefinitely after the real feature becomes canonical.

Once the production implementation is accepted:
- identify obsolete runtime branches and related translations/tests;
- report them as cleanup candidates;
- replace obsolete tests with regression tests for canonical behavior when cleanup is explicitly approved;
- do not remove anything automatically.

## Feature flags

A feature flag is not permission to preserve obsolete implementations forever.

After rollout is accepted and rollback risk is reasonably closed:
- identify the retired branch and flag as cleanup candidates;
- verify that no production path depends on them;
- preserve rollback capability through Git;
- remove only after explicit approval.

## Safety before any proposed deletion

Before recommending code for deletion:
1. search all references/imports;
2. inspect runtime and framework entry points;
3. check dynamic/string-based references;
4. check tests;
5. check locale keys/config references;
6. check Supabase, Vercel, cron, webhook, route, migration and deployment dependencies where relevant;
7. establish the current baseline behavior for medium/high/critical-risk code.

Cleanup must not include unrelated refactoring.

## Agent reporting

When legacy code is encountered during a task, report:
- what appears canonical;
- what appears superseded;
- confidence and risk;
- what evidence supports the classification;
- what compatibility code remains and why;
- what separate cleanup action is recommended.

Do not claim obsolete code was removed unless the user explicitly approved that removal and the required verification was completed.
